        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Etruscans</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> (Tuscany, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>), with later cities in the Po valley and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 900 BCE – 1st century BCE; the Villanovan culture from c. 900 BCE, the height of the city-states in the 7th–5th centuries BCE, conquest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 4th–3rd centuries BCE and absorption into Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by the time of Augustus</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Painted tombs at Tarquinia and the tomb cities of Cerveteri; terracottas and bronzes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-of-veii">Apollo of Veii</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimera-of-arezzo">Chimera of Arezzo</a>; a non-Indo-European language; divination by haruspices; their influence on early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated cities, sanctuaries and above all cemeteries; some 13,000 inscriptions in Etruscan, most of them short, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi-tablets">Pyrgi Tablets</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liber-linteus">Liber Linteus</a>; Greek and Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and Dionysius of Halicarnassus</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">10 min read · Last reviewed October 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Etruscans of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> built independent city-states such as Veii, Tarquinia, Caere and Vulci, rich from metals, farming and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> from the 8th century BCE.</li><li>They spoke a language unrelated to Latin or Greek, wrote it in an alphabet taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and are known above all from their tombs, with wall paintings, sculpture and rich grave goods.</li><li>By Roman tradition Etruscan kings ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 6th century BCE; from the capture of Veii in 396 BCE Rome conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> city by city and absorbed its people in the 1st century BCE.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The longest known Etruscan text, probably a ritual calendar, written on a linen book, survives only because the cloth was cut into strips to wrap an Egyptian mummy, now in the Archaeological Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

