    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 900 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Villanovan culture of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> appears on the sites of the later Etruscan cities</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 700 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The first Etruscan inscriptions are written in an alphabet adapted from the Greek</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">616–509 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">By Roman tradition, Etruscan kings rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 540 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Etruscan and Carthaginian ships fight the Phocaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">474 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-i-of-syracuse">Hiero I of Syracuse</a> destroys an Etruscan fleet off Cumae</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">396 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> captures Veii after a long siege</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">264 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sacks Volsinii and moves its people to a new site</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">90 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> the Etruscans receive Roman citizenship</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/regolini-galassi-tomb"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/regolini-galassi-tomb.jpg" alt="Regolini-Galassi tomb" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/regolini-galassi-tomb">Regolini-Galassi tomb</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grande_fibula_d%27oro_con_staffa_a_disco,_da_tomba_regolini-galassi_di_cerveteri,_675-650_ac._ca.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Who the Etruscans were and where they came from was debated already in antiquity. The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, in the 5th century BCE, told how half the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, driven out by famine, sailed west under Tyrrhenus, son of their king, and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, taking his name. Dionysius of Halicarnassus, writing under Augustus, rejected the story and held that the Etruscans were native to Italy, adding that they called themselves Rasenna. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called them Tyrrhenians or Tyrsenians, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> Etrusci or Tusci, a name that survives in Tuscany.</p><p>Most archaeologists now see the Etruscans as a people formed in Italy. From about 900 BCE the Villanovan culture of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, named after a cemetery at Villanova near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> excavated in 1853, appears on the plateaus where the great Etruscan cities later stood. Its people cremated their dead and buried the ashes in urns, some covered with helmets, others made in the shape of huts. Nothing marks a large migration at its start, and the Villanovan villages grew steadily into the Etruscan cities. A study of ancient DNA published in 2021 found the Etruscans of the 8th to 1st centuries BCE genetically similar to their Latin neighbours, with no sign of recent ancestry from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> or the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><p>Their language remains the puzzle. Unlike Latin, Umbrian and Greek, it is not Indo-European, and its only known relatives are Lemnian, known chiefly from a 6th-century BCE inscription on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, and Raetic, written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. Scholars debate whether it was a survivor of the languages spoken in Italy before the spread of Indo-European tongues, as the genetic evidence suggests, or came by sea.</p><p>The 8th century BCE brought rapid change. Greek traders and settlers, first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithekoussai">Pithekoussai</a> on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> and then at Cumae, came for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and iron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>’s hills and of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>. Wealth from metals, farming and trade created an aristocracy whose princely tombs of the Orientalising period, from about 720 to 580 BCE, were filled with gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, bronze and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> in styles from the eastern Mediterranean, as in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regolini-galassi-tomb">Regolini-Galassi tomb</a> at Caere of about 675–650 BCE. Around 700 BCE the Etruscans began to write, adapting the alphabet of the Euboean Greeks, and the scattered villages on each plateau merged into cities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sarcophagus-of-the-spouses"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarcophagus-of-the-spouses.jpg" alt="Sarcophagus of the Spouses" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sarcophagus-of-the-spouses">Sarcophagus of the Spouses</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarcophagus_of_the_Spouses_from_Villa_Giulia_in_Rome.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> was never a single state. Its cities, among them Veii, Caere (Cerveteri), Tarquinii (Tarquinia), Vulci, Volsinii, Clusium (Chiusi), Perusia (Perugia), Arretium (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>), Cortona, Volaterrae (Volterra), Vetulonia, Rusellae and Populonia, were independent, each ruling its own territory and often at odds with the others. Roman writers describe a league of twelve peoples whose delegates met each year at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fanum-voltumnae">Fanum Voltumnae</a>, the sanctuary of Voltumna near Volsinii, for festivals, games and councils, but the league rarely acted together in war; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, the other cities left Veii to face <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> alone.</p><p>The early cities were ruled by kings; by the 5th century BCE most were governed by aristocratic families, who held the magistracies, the highest of them called zilath. Below the aristocracy were craftsmen, traders and farmers, and a large population of dependants and slaves, whose revolt at Volsinii in the 260s BCE gave Rome the occasion to intervene.</p><p>Etruscan women enjoyed a freedom that surprised Greek and Roman observers. They reclined beside their husbands at banquets, as on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarcophagus-of-the-spouses">Sarcophagus of the Spouses</a> from Caere of about 520 BCE, appear in inscriptions with their own first names, such as Larthia, Ramtha and Thanchvil, and many epitaphs name the dead by their mother’s family as well as their father’s. The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theopompus">Theopompus</a>, in the 4th century BCE, described their freedom with open hostility, while Roman tradition made Tanaquil, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>, a skilled reader of omens who helped to make two kings.</p><p>The fertile volcanic soils produced wheat, olives and vines, and Etruscan wine travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphora">amphorae</a> to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 7th and 6th centuries BCE. At Populonia furnaces smelted iron ore from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a>, leaving heaps of slag so large that they were mined again in the 20th century. Craftsmen worked bronze and gold and made bucchero, a glossy black pottery typical of Etruria. Engineers drained land with underground channels, cut roads through the soft volcanic tuff, and laid out new cities such as Marzabotto, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, on a regular grid in the late 6th century BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/liver-of-piacenza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liver-of-piacenza.jpg" alt="Liver of Piacenza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/liver-of-piacenza">Liver of Piacenza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piacenza_liver.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lokilech, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> described the Etruscans as a people devoted to religion above all others, the more so because they excelled in the art of practising it. Their religion rested on sacred books, the Etrusca disciplina, revealed according to tradition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tages">Tages</a>, a child prophet who sprang from a ploughed furrow near Tarquinia, and by the nymph Vegoia. The books taught how to read the entrails of sacrificed animals and the meaning of lightning, and set out rituals for founding cities, marking boundaries and averting misfortune.</p><p>The priests called haruspices read the will of the gods in the livers of sacrificed sheep. A bronze model of a liver found near Piacenza in 1877, made around 100 BCE, is divided into regions inscribed with the names of gods, matching the division of the sky, which the Etruscans split into sixteen parts in order to judge where lightning came from. Roman generals and senators consulted haruspices for centuries.</p><p>Among the gods were Tinia, lord of the sky and lightning, Uni, Menrva, Turan, goddess of love, Fufluns, god of wine, Turms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sethlans">Sethlans</a>, the smith god, and Hercle. From the 6th century BCE many of them were identified with Greek gods, Tinia with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, Uni with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, Menrva with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, Fufluns with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, and bronze mirrors engraved with Greek myths label the figures with Etruscan names. Temples stood on high podiums with steps only at the front, a deep porch of columns and often three rooms at the back; their mud-brick walls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> roofs were decorated with painted terracotta, like the life-size statues on the roof of the Portonaccio temple at Veii, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-of-veii">Apollo of Veii</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi">Pyrgi</a>, the port of Caere, gold tablets of about 500 BCE record in Etruscan and Phoenician a dedication by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thefarie-velianas">Thefarie Velianas</a>, ruler of Caere, to Uni, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> in the Phoenician text.</p><p>The Etruscans built cities of the dead beside their cities of the living. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banditaccia-necropolis">Banditaccia necropolis</a> at Cerveteri, hundreds of earth-covered mounds cover chambers cut in the rock like houses, with beds, chairs and carved roof beams; the Tomb of the Reliefs, of the 4th century BCE, has walls covered with painted stucco tools, weapons and household objects. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monterozzi-necropolis">Monterozzi necropolis</a> at Tarquinia some two hundred painted tombs show banquets, musicians, dancers, athletes, hunting and fishing; later paintings show demons of the underworld, such as Charun with his hammer and the winged Vanth, escorting the dead.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/apollo-of-veii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apollo-of-veii.jpg" alt="Apollo of Veii" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/apollo-of-veii">Apollo of Veii</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GiorcesVeiiApollo1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Etruscan art drew on Greek and Near Eastern models but kept a character of its own. Etruscan bronze-workers were famous in antiquity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimera-of-arezzo">Chimera of Arezzo</a>, of about 400 BCE, found in 1553 and inscribed as a gift to Tinia, and the Mars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todi">Todi</a>, of about the same date, are among the finest ancient bronzes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, while the Orator, a life-size bronze of about 100 BCE, shows an Etruscan nobleman, named in its Etruscan inscription as Aule Metele, dressed in a Roman toga. The bronze Capitoline Wolf, long admired as Etruscan work of the 5th century BCE, has been dated by recent studies of its casting to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, though the question is still debated.</p><p>Terracotta sculptors worked on a large scale. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vulca">Vulca</a> of Veii is the only Etruscan artist named in Roman literature, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-of-veii">Apollo of Veii</a> is often attributed to his workshop. Goldsmiths mastered granulation, decorating jewellery with thousands of tiny gold grains, and engravers decorated bronze mirrors with scenes from myth. Potters made their own versions of Corinthian and Athenian painted wares, and later cinerary urns of alabaster and tuff with carved scenes, of which Volterra’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarnacci-museum">Guarnacci Museum</a> holds some six hundred. The Etruscans were also among the greatest buyers of Athenian painted pottery, and many of the finest Greek vases, such as the François Vase, found near Chiusi in 1844, survived in their tombs.</p><p>Wall painting survives in the tombs: the scenes of feasting and sport at Tarquinia, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-tomb">François Tomb</a> at Vulci, of the 4th century BCE, which sets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>’ sacrifice of Trojan prisoners beside local heroes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastarna">Macstrna</a> freeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caelius-vibenna">Caelius Vibenna</a>. Builders raised city walls of fitted stone and, in the 3rd and 2nd centuries BCE, gates with true arches, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arco-etrusco">Arco Etrusco</a> at Perugia.</p><p>Etruscan, written mostly from right to left in the alphabet adopted around 700 BCE, can be read aloud, and much of its grammar and part of its vocabulary are known from bilingual texts, glosses in ancient writers and comparison of the 13,000 or so inscriptions, most of them short epitaphs and dedications. Longer texts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liber-linteus">Liber Linteus</a>, the Tabula Capuana and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cippus-perusinus">Cippus Perusinus</a> are understood only in part. Etruscan literature, including histories and ritual books, is lost, as is the history of the Etruscans in twenty books written by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. The Etruscans passed the alphabet on to other peoples of Italy, and the Latin alphabet probably came from a Greek model by way of Etruscan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi-tablets"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyrgi-tablets.jpg" alt="Pyrgi Tablets" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi-tablets">Pyrgi Tablets</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lamine_d'oro_in_lingua_etrusca_e_fenicia_con_dedica_di_un_luogo_sacro_a_pyrgi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sailko, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Etruscans traded and fought across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> grew from the metal trade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithekoussai">Pithekoussai</a>; Roman tradition told of Demaratus, a nobleman of Corinth, who settled at Tarquinia with craftsmen, and from the early 6th century BCE Greek traders had a sanctuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gravisca">Gravisca</a>, the port of Tarquinia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> were partners too. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> mentions treaties between the Etruscans and Carthage on trade and alliance, and around 540 BCE their fleets fought the Phocaean Greeks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalia">Alalia</a> off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says that the Greeks won but lost so many ships that they abandoned the island.</p><p>In the 6th century BCE Etruscan power reached from the Po valley, where Felsina (the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>), the new town of Marzabotto and the Adriatic port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spina">Spina</a> flourished, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, around Capua. Then it declined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristodemus-of-cumae">Aristodemus of Cumae</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latins">Latins</a> defeated an Etruscan army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aricia">Aricia</a> around 504 BCE, and in 474 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-i-of-syracuse">Hiero I of Syracuse</a>, allied with Cumae, destroyed an Etruscan fleet off Cumae, dedicating captured helmets at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>, one of them now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Samnites took Capua in the late 5th century BCE, Celtic peoples overran the Po valley, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I of Syracuse</a> sacked the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi">Pyrgi</a> in 384 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> lay on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>’s southern border, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>. By Roman tradition its fifth king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>, came from Tarquinia, the sixth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servius-tullius">Servius Tullius</a>, was identified by the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> with the Etruscan adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastarna">Mastarna</a>, and the last, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a>, was expelled about 509 BCE, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lars-porsenna">Lars Porsenna</a> of Clusium attacked the city and may have taken it. Rome learned much from Etruria: the temple of Jupiter on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>, built with the help of Etruscan craftsmen, the draining of the Forum, and symbols of office such as the fasces.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> then expanded at its neighbours’ expense. Veii fell in 396 BCE after a long siege led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-furius-camillus">Marcus Furius Camillus</a>; Tarquinia fought Rome in the 350s BCE; Etruscan cities backed the Samnites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> defeated at Sentinum in 295 BCE; and Rome won again at Lake Vadimo in 283 BCE. In 264 BCE Rome sacked Volsinii and moved its people to a new site. By then every Etruscan city was bound to Rome by treaty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chimera-of-arezzo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chimera-of-arezzo.jpg" alt="Chimera of Arezzo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chimera-of-arezzo">Chimera of Arezzo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chimera_d%27arezzo,_fi,_04.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sailko, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the Etruscan cities kept their own laws and magistrates but supplied troops and resources. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> prepared his invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 205 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> records, Caere sent grain, Populonia iron, Tarquinia linen for sails and Arretium shields, helmets and spears. Roman colonies and roads, such as the Via Aurelia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-cassia">Via Cassia</a>, spread Latin through the countryside.</p><p>The end of Etruscan independence came in the 1st century BCE. In 90 BCE, after the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a>, Rome granted citizenship to the allies who had not rebelled, among them the Etruscans, and their cities became Roman municipalities. Many Etruscans then backed the party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, who punished them by besieging Volaterrae for about two years, confiscating land and settling veterans at Faesulae and elsewhere. In 41–40 BCE Octavian besieged and burned Perusia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perusine-war">Perusine War</a>. Latin replaced Etruscan in public life; bilingual epitaphs show families changing language, and the last Etruscan inscriptions date from around the turn of the eras. Augustus made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> the seventh region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Etruscan nobles such as his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-maecenas">Gaius Maecenas</a>, from an old family of Arretium, entered the Roman elite.</p><p>Etruscan religion outlived the language. In 47 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> urged the Senate to preserve the college of haruspices so that the oldest art of Italy should not die out, and Etruscan diviners offered their services to Rome as late as 408 CE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> of Alaric besieged the city.</p><p>Modern interest began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimera-of-arezzo">Chimera of Arezzo</a> was found, and grew after Thomas Dempster’s De Etruria regali was published in 1723–1726; the Accademia Etrusca of Cortona followed in 1727. Josiah Wedgwood named his pottery works Etruria in 1769, in the mistaken belief that Greek vases from Etruscan tombs were Etruscan. Excavators and treasure hunters, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucien-bonaparte">Lucien Bonaparte</a> at Vulci, emptied thousands of tombs in the 19th century, and George Dennis’s The Cities and Cemeteries of Etruria (1848) brought the Etruscans to a wide public. Twentieth-century scholars such as Massimo Pallottino replaced the romantic image of a mysterious people with that of an Italian civilisation formed in Italy, known from its cities, cemeteries and own words.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Etruscans of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> built rich, independent city-states from about 900 BCE, traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>, and created a distinctive art of painted tombs, bronzes and terracottas. They left a deep mark on early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which conquered their cities in the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE and absorbed their people, while their religion and arts influenced Roman civilisation for centuries.</em></p>

